{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Command the children of Israel, etc.</b> One needs to know why He arranged the section of the offerings in this place, after the appointment of Joshua, and not in the place where He commanded about the offerings in the section of Emor — for this commandment you are compelled to say was practiced from the time the Tabernacle was erected, whereas Joshua was appointed in the fortieth year." "Or it may be that it comes to say that if Joshua wished to bring tamid and musaf offerings from his own property, he is not authorized, even though the law of the community applies to him, as is compelling from several places. Therefore He arranged this commandment after the appointment of Joshua, and commanded in it that it be from the community's property, as He said: ״צַו אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. — to say that the tamid offerings come only from the community's property. And for this reason He repeated, saying <b>and you shall say to them</b>, as a requirement. And examine also His saying <b>to them</b> (אֲלֵיהֶם) and not 'to them' (לָהֶם) — according to what I explained in the section ״וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו״ (Genesis 18:1), that אֶל implies exclusion of anything besides, so too in what is before us He alerted us that His intent is to exclude. I subsequently found in the words of our Rabbis (Yalkut Shimoni) who said as follows, and this is their language: Because Israel would say — in the past the journeys were practiced and the tamid offerings were practiced; now that the journeys have ceased, the tamid offerings have ceased. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Tell them that the tamid offerings shall continue to be practiced — end of their words. And there are seventy faces to the Torah." "<b>My offering, my food, etc.</b> Meaning, since the tamid offerings comprise three kinds of offering: first — the offering of the tamid animal itself; second — the tenth of an ephah of the meal offering of the tamid; third — the libations. Corresponding to the offering of the tamid He said <b>My offering</b>; corresponding to the meal offering He said <b>My food</b>, for it is called לֶחֶם (bread), and both of them go entirely to the fires, therefore He said <b>for My fire</b>; and corresponding to the libations He said <b>a pleasing fragrance to Me</b> — for there is nothing in them but fragrance, since they are not for the fires but are poured on the foundation and descend into the drains. And He was precise in using the possessive suffix for three things: My offering, My food, My fragrance — to say that all of them belong to the Holy One, blessed be He, the Creator of all, and are still in His possession, as it says (Psalms 24:1) ״לַה׳ הָאָרֶץ וּמְלוֹאָהּ״ — which we explained in its place. And He was precise also in saying <b>for My fire</b> with the possessive suffix, to say that even the fire belongs to the Holy One, blessed be He, and consequently what Israel bring is from the property of the Creator." "<b>For this reason</b> HaShem was wise and gave a warning and said <b>you shall observe</b>, to say that if they do not bring them, behold they are stealing His property and transgressing a negative commandment. And His saying <b>to offer to Me</b> etc. means that what they do is the offering of a portion of His property before Him, but the principal of the property belongs to the Omnipresent." "<b>Further,</b> He alluded in this to reveal the sod of the offering, which is to bring near the branches of holiness that are the portion of HaShem — and this is what He alluded to in saying <b>My offering</b> with the possessive suffix referring to Himself, and understand. And His saying <b>My food for My fire</b> means: this bread that I command to bring to the fires — its sod is My offering, for when a pleasing fragrance ascends to HaShem, all the sparks of holiness that had become distanced are drawn near."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {1 "The distinction between אֲלֵיהֶם and לָהֶם depends on the Hebrew prepositions אל versus ל; both mean 'to them' in English but the Or HaChaim reads אל as carrying a sense of exclusion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:54.724715590Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}